h Canada to voluntarily discontinue the marketing authorizations for RELYVRIO®/ALBRIOZA™ (sodium phenylbutyrate and taurursodiol [also known as ... As of September 30, 2024, we have funded our operations primarily through public offerings of our common stock, private sales of preferred stock, convertible notes, and through revenue from sales of RELYVRIO and ALBRIOZA in the U.S. and Canada, respectively.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRelyvrio ALS Regulatory FDA Approval Timeline | Inflection Labs (https://www.inflectionlabs.bio/reports/relyvrio-fda-approval-review)\nciteturn138search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Relyvrio received FDA approval on September 29, 2022. ... [4]“Clinical and Statistical Review — NDA 216660 (AMX0035 / RELYVRIO)” · FDA Center for Drug Evaluation and Research — Veneeta Tandon (Clinical), Tristan Massie (Statistics) · September 28, 2022 View source\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndnli-20200630 (https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1714899/000171489920000092/dnli-20200630.htm)\nciteturn138search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.3 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; We have also developed a proprietary BBB platform technology, our transport vehicle (\"TV\"), which enables multiple modality-based platforms to deliver a wide range of large-molecule therapeutics across the BBB, including enzymes, antibodies, proteins and oligonucleotides. ... EIF2B  |  | DNL343  |  | Ph 1  |  | ALS and FTD  |  | Denali ... •In June 2020, we announced the results from Phase 1b clinical studies with small molecule RIPK1 inhibitor DNL747 in Alzheimer’s disease and ALS, and provided a broad RIPK1 program update including CNS compound DNL788 and peripherally-restricted compound DNL758. Safety endpoints were met in the Phase 1b patient studies with DNL747 in ALS and Alzheimer’s disease, however further dose escalation to achieve higher levels of target inhibition may be limited by preclinical chronic safety data.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDocument (https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1714899/000171489920000091/ex991pressreleaseq22020.htm)\nciteturn138search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.3 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; •RIPK1 CNS program update - In June 2020, together with partner Sanofi, Denali announced results from Phase 1b clinical studies with small molecule RIPK1 inhibitor DNL747 in Alzheimer’s disease and ALS.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nbiib-20241231 (https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/875045/000087504525000009/biib-20241231.htm)\nciteturn138search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: today; In May 2024 we and Ionis announced that the Phase 1/2 ALSpire study of BIIB105, an investigational ASO for the potential treatment of ALS, did not meet its endpoints. ... In early 2025 we discontinued further development of BIIB143 (cemdomespib) for the treatment of diabetic neuropathic pain, as part of our ongoing pipeline prioritization efforts.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMaker of much-debated ALS drug says it may stop selling it after study showed it didn't work (https://apnews.com/article/7340ef880306fbbadad720b8154b3605)\nciteturn138news20 [wordlim: 100] Published: 2.7 years ago; Date: Fri Mar  8 16:30:21 2024 UTC ... The Food and Drug Administration approved Amylyx Pharmaceuticals' Relyvrio in September 2022, following a years-long advocacy campaign by patients with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, or ALS, a fatal muscle-wasting disease. ... Amylyx's medication is part of a string of drugs for deadly, degenerative diseases that have won FDA approval in recent years despite questionable proof that they work.\nMaker of much-debated ALS drug says it may stop selling it after study showed it didn't work\nDate: Fri Mar  8 16:30:21 2024 UTC\nLocation: Washington, District of Columbia, United States\n\nWASHINGTON (AP) - The maker of a much-debated drug for Lou Gehrig's disease said Friday its therapy failed to help patients in a large follow-up study, but stopped short of committing to follow through on a prior pledge to pull the drug from the U.S. market.\nThe Food and Drug Administration approved Amylyx Pharmaceuticals' Relyvrio in September 2022, following a years-long advocacy campaign by patients with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, or ALS, a fatal muscle-wasting disease.\nAmylyx said Friday it would discuss its plans for Relyvrio with patients and the FDA, which \"may include voluntarily withdrawing\" the drug. Executives said in a statement they were \"surprised and disappointed\" by the results and hoped to announce their plans in the next two months.\nThe latest company study showed that the drug did not slow the disease compared with a dummy treatment. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nALS drug will be pulled from US market after study showed patients didn't benefit (https://apnews.com/article/5fca44189f7e33f7645cb8d019197442)\nciteturn138news21 [wordlim: 100] Published: 2.6 years ago; Date: Thu Apr  4 14:05:59 2024 UTC ... The Food and Drug Administration approved the much-debated drug, Relyvrio, in September 2022, following a years-long advocacy campaign by patients with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, or ALS. ... That's because the FDA granted the drug full approval, despite the preliminary nature of the company's data on effectiveness.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNew ALS treatment sparks yet another drug pricing debate (https://www.axios.com/2022/10/03/als-treatment-fda-drug-prices)\nciteturn138news22 [wordlim: 100] Published: 4.1 years ago; Date: Mon Oct  3 09:30:33 2022 UTC ... Driving the news: The FDA on Thursday announced the approval of Relyvrio, an experimental drug combination from Amylyx Pharmaceuticals. ... The big picture: The entire episode recalls last year's controversial FDA approval of Aduhelm, an Alzheimer's treatment that also had limited evidence that it worked. ... Editor's note: This story has been updated to incorporate a more comprehensive account of the Amylyx co-CEO's remarks on an investor call.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSetbacks reset views on highly touted Alzheimer's and ALS drugs (https://www.axios.com/2024/03/11/setbacks-drugs-als-alzheimers-fda)\nciteturn138news23 [wordlim: 100] Published: 2.7 years ago; Date: Mon Mar 11 09:50:00 2024 UTC ... Driving the news: Amylyx Pharmaceuticals on Friday said Relyvrio, its drug for ALS, or Lou Gehrig's Disease, failed a post-approval trial of 664 patients by not showing a significant difference over a placebo. \nAlso on Friday, Eli Lilly said the FDA said it would delay action on the company's experimental Alzheimer's drug donanemab while it convenes outside advisers to review its safety and efficacy.\n"
